type": "secondary_8", "content": "Understands important information and aspects of attitude and intention in spoken situations in English on a range of common communicative topics related to this level. "}]}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn5le05ob03re1img01.jpg"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Simple Past Tense"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "When you make questions and negatives in the Simple Past tense, you use <span class=\"italic\">did</span>."}, {"type": "text", "content": " Questions are formed by adding did before the subject and the verb.<br/> Example: <span class=\"italic\">When did he go into the woods?</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": " Negatives are formed by adding did and not after the subject.<br/> Example: <span class=\"italic\">They didn't keep any mirrors in their home.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Remember that when we use the auxiliary did or didn't the verb does not have to be written in past."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Correct:<Br/> <span class=\"italic\"> What did Narcissus say to the nymph?<br/> Narcissus didn't  love the nymph.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": " Incorrect:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">What did Narcissus said to the nymph?<br/>  Narcissus didn't love the nymph.</span>"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Quantifiers"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use a quantifier before a noun to indicate the amount or number of an item."}, {"type": "example", "content": " They didn't keep any mirrors in their home.  "}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":2,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Quantifier\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Meaning\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"more<br> less/fewer<br> no/none <br>any /not any <br>some <br>a lot of / lots of <br>plenty of <br>enough\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\